\details{
Fuzzy numbers are represented through their \code{alpha}-level sets
\verb{[X^-(alpha), X^+(alpha)]}, and every statistical operator is defined
level-wise, so that the \strong{expectation}, \strong{variance}, \strong{covariance},
\strong{empirical distribution}, \strong{law of large numbers} and \strong{central limit
theorem} are all handled in a single coherent framework.

The key design choices are:
\itemize{
\item A \emph{fuzzy number} is a list of \code{alpha} levels and the matching
non-decreasing \code{lower} / non-increasing \code{upper} envelopes
(class \code{FuzzyNumber}).
\item A \emph{sample of fuzzy random variables} is an \verb{(n, m, 2)} array of cuts
(class \code{FuzzySample}), \code{m} being the number of levels.
\item The covariance of two fuzzy random variables is returned as a
level-wise \verb{2 x 2} matrix of covariances between the lower and upper
endpoints (class \code{FuzzyCovariance}); scalar reductions are provided
through the Feng-Hu-Shu integral and scalar product.
\item The central limit theorem yields the \emph{fuzzy normal} distribution
(class \code{FuzzyNormal}), whose level cuts are bivariate-normal intervals.
}

The package only depends on base R (\code{stats}, \code{graphics}).
